---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import WordSearchTool from "../tools/WordSearchTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="wordsearch">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Your own words hidden in a grid of letters, ready to engrave. Every word on the printed list is
        <strong class="text-slate-300">actually in the grid</strong> — the ones that would not fit are taken off the
        list rather than left there for somebody to hunt for.
    </Fragment>

    <WordSearchTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </WordSearchTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The one unforgivable bug</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A word search that asks for a word it does not contain. Somebody looks for twenty minutes, and the
                    answer is that the generator tried a couple of hundred random spots for the fourth word, gave up,
                    and printed it on the list anyway.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So the placement here is exhaustive rather than hopeful: every starting square in every allowed
                    direction, shuffled, and the best one wins. If a word genuinely cannot go in — it is longer than
                    the grid, or the grid is full — it is <em>dropped from the list too</em>, and the status bar says
                    which. There is no third outcome.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Crossings, not corners</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Among all the places a word fits, the one that shares the most letters with words already
                        placed wins. A grid whose words cross each other is dense and hard; one where each word was
                        dropped into the first empty corner has a suspicious blank middle, and the eye goes straight
                        to the letters that are doing something.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The filler gives it away</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Pad the gaps from the whole alphabet and the puzzle solves itself: a Q or an X says there is
                        nothing to find there. Drawing the filler from the words' <em>own</em> letters is the single
                        biggest thing that makes a grid hard — with it, every letter looks like it might be the start
                        of something.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Monospaced, or it is not a grid</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The letters are set by the browser's own shaper, which packs an I tighter than a W. In a
                        proportional face the columns wander and the grid stops being one. Any monospaced font on your
                        machine works; the system default is picked for you.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The letters come from the text tool</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Not a second glyph pipeline. This tool composes a string — the title, the grid, the word list —
                    and hands it to the same machinery that sets a keychain: rendered large in your chosen font and
                    traced, so the outline is accurate to the figure in the status bar. It means a word search gets
                    every font on your machine, the same "cap height, not font size" sizing as everything else, and
                    any improvement to the tracer for free.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Everything goes in capitals, which in German makes a word with an <em>ß</em> one letter longer than it
                was typed — "GRÖSSE" is six squares, not five. Below about 4 mm an engraved capital starts to close up,
                so a big grid wants a big piece rather than smaller letters.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
